<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Solution-wide NuGet configuration.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
  ====================
  NuGetAuditMode=all is mandatory on this stack (STACK.md §11, R7, T-RULE 015) and
  must stay ON. With auditing enabled, every `dotnet restore` downloads the
  vulnerability database from the configured audit source. When no <auditSources>
  is declared, NuGet falls back to the *full* package feed
  (https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json). On a slow / filtered / proxied network
  that full feed can stall and produce:

      "The download of 'https://api.nuget.org/v3-vulnerabilities/.../vulnerability.base.json'
       timed out because no data was received for 60000ms."

  FIX (Microsoft-recommended, audit stays ON)
  ===========================================
  Point auditing at the dedicated *vulnerability-data-only* service index
  https://data.nuget.org/v3/index.json. It serves only the advisory database
  (much smaller than the full feed), so restore is faster and far less likely to
  time out, while the audit guarantee required by the supply-chain rules is kept.
  Ref: https://learn.microsoft.com/nuget/concepts/auditing-packages (auditSources)

  This changes NOTHING about which packages restore (packageSources is the same
  default nuget.org feed) and does NOT disable or weaken auditing — it only makes
  the audit data come from the lighter, purpose-built endpoint.

  ABOUT THE NU1900 / NU1905 WARNINGS
  ==================================
  NU1900 ("Error occurred while getting package vulnerability data: The download of
  '.../vulnerability.base.json' timed out ...") and the NU1905 that follows it
  ("Audit source 'nuget.org-audit' did not provide any vulnerability data") are NOT
  reports of a discovered vulnerability. They mean the audit database could not be
  fetched *this run* — a transient network condition (slow/filtered/proxied link
  reaching the hosted advisory service). They are therefore environmental, not code
  or configuration defects, and the correct handling is:

    * Keep NuGetAuditMode=all ON (STACK.md §11, R7, T-RULE 015) — never silence the
      audit to make the warning disappear; that would remove the supply-chain check.
    * Do NOT add a <NuGetAuditSuppress>: suppression is reserved for a *named*
      advisory ID with a dated justification (T-RULE 015). NU1900/NU1905 carry no
      advisory and must not be suppressed.
    * Re-run restore on a healthy connection (the advisory DB is cached once fetched,
      so the warning clears on the next successful download). CI restores locked
      (RestorePackagesWithLockFile=true) so package resolution itself stays
      deterministic regardless of this transient audit fetch.
-->
<configuration>

  <!-- Package restore feed: the standard nuget.org feed (explicit = same as the
       implicit default, kept here so the source set is deterministic). -->
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>

  <!-- Dedicated audit feed: vulnerability data only (small + fast), so
       NuGetAuditMode=all no longer pulls the heavy full index and no longer
       times out on constrained networks. -->
  <auditSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org-audit" value="https://data.nuget.org/v3/index.json" />
  </auditSources>

</configuration>
